</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-derivatives-interp" >
	<title>Interpreting Functions and Derivatives in Context</title>

	<p>While graphing produces nifty images from functions, the derivatives can also be used to interpret functions in context.</p>

	<example xml:id="example-derivative-interpret-distance">
		<title>Interpreting Distance</title>

		<p>Consider the function <m>s(t)</m> which represents the distance of a person from home at a time given in minutes.</p>
		<p>If <m>s'(t) \gt 0</m> for <m>t \in [0,5]</m>, then the person's distance from home is increasing. Phrased simply the person is going away from home for those five minutes.</p>
		<p>If <m>s''(t) \lt 0</m> and <m>s'(t) \gt 0</m> for <m>t \in [10,15]</m>, then the person is getting farther away from home (1st derivative) but slowing down (2nd derivative).</p>
		<p>If <m>s'(t)=0</m> for <m>t \in [20,25]</m> then the person is not moving for those five minutes (might be practicing social distancing).</p>
		<p>If <m>s''(t) \lt 0</m> and <m>s'(t) \lt 0</m> for <m>t \in [30,35]</m>, the person is returning home and increasing speed toward home. Perhaps the person is going to celebrate the great grade on a calculus exam.</p>
	</example>

	<example xml:id="example-derivative-interpret-demand">
		<title>Interpreting Product Demand</title>

		<p>Consider the function <m>d(t)</m> which represents the number of widgets sold on day <m>t</m>.</p>
		<p>If <m>d'(t) \gt 0</m> for <m>t \in [0,5]</m>, then the number of widgets sold per day is increasing. Demand is increasing.</p>
		<p>If <m>d''(t) \lt 0</m> and <m>d'(t) \gt 0</m> for <m>t \in [10,15]</m>, then the number of widgets sold each day is increasing (1st derivative), but each day the increase is smaller (2nd derivative).</p>
		<p>If <m>d'(t)=0</m> for <m>t \in [20,25]</m> then the number of widgets being sold is not changing. Note this does not mean none are sold: it could be that 20 are sold each day because that is no change in demand.</p>
		<p>If <m>d''(t) \lt 0</m> and <m>d'(t) \lt 0</m> for <m>t \in [30,35]</m>, the number of widgets sold each day is decreasing greater each day. I hope you sold your stock in this company.</p>
	</example>

</subsection>

</section>
